trowthe, < AS. ''treówthu'', ''treówth'' (''treówth-'', ''treóth-'') (= OHG. *''triuwida'', in comp., = Icel. ''tryggth''), truth, faith; with formative ''-th'', < ''treówe'', true: see ''true''.] '''1.''' The state or character of being true; trueness. {{smaller|(''a'') Conformity of thought with fact; conformity of a judgment, statement, or belief with the reality; exact correspondence of subjective and objective relations.}}
{{smaller block|All admit that by ''truth'' is understood a harmony, an agreement, a correspondence between our thought and that which we think about. This definition we owe to the schoolmen. “Veritas intellectus,” says Aquinas, “est adæquatio intellectus et rei, secundum quod intellectus dicit esse quod est, vel non esse quod non est.”
{{right|''Sir W. Hamilton'', Logic, xxvii.}}}}
{{smaller block|In common life we call ''truth'' the agreement between an object and our conception of the object. We thus presuppose an object to which our conception must conform. In the philosophical sense of the word, on the other hand, ''truth'' may be described, in a general and one-sided way, as the agreement of the subject-matter of thought with itself.
{{right|''Hegel'', Logic (tr. by Wallace), p. 43.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The state of being made true or exact; exact conformity to a model, rule, or plan; accuracy of adjustment; exact adaptation.}}
{{smaller block|Ploughs, to go true, depend much on the ''truth'' of the iron-work.
{{right|''Mortimer'', Husbandry.}}}}
{{smaller block|Most gun-stocks are twisted over — that is to say, the toe of the butt is more out of ''truth'' with the barrels than the heel.
{{right|''W. W. Greener'', The Gun, p. 432.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') In the ''fine arts'', the proper and correct representation of any object in nature, or of whatever subject may be under treatment; specifically, in ''arch.'', avoidance of deceits in construction or decoration, as of non-concordance of apparent and real structure, or of imitation of stone or marble in paint or plaster.}}
{{smaller block|The agony of the Laocoon, the action of the Discobulus, the upspringing of the Mercury, are all apparently real in their action by the innate ''truth'' of their conformation. . . . ''Truth'' is therefore the highest quality in Art.}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Fairholt'', Dict. Terms of Art.}}
In ''truth'' and skill of modelling even the sculptures of Chartres and St. Denis, which are a century earlier in date, surpass those of Wells.
{{right|''C. H. Moore'', Gothic Architecture, p. 286.}}}}
(''d'') Habitual disposition to speak only what is true; veracity; purity from falsehood; truthfulness; sincerity; uprightness; honesty: as, a man of ''truth.''
{{smaller block|For als longe as zee ben bounden to gedere in places — that is to seyne, in Love, in ''Trouthe,'' and in gode Accord — no man schalle ben of powere to greve zou.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 229.}}
Love is all ''truth.'' {{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 804.}}}}
(''e'') Disposition to be faithful; fidelity; constancy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Long since we were resolved of your ''truth.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iii. 4. 20.}}
<poem>Now I shall try thy ''truth.'' If thou dost love me,
Thou weigh’st not any thing compar’d with me.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid’s Tragedy, ii. 1.}}
<poem>Alas! they had been friends in youth;
But whispering tongues can poison ''truth.''</poem>
{{right|''Coleridge'', Christabel, ii.}}}}
(''f'') The state of not being counterfeited or adulterated; genuineness; purity.
{{smaller block|The ''truth'' of thy love to me.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 2. 13.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is true. (''a'') Fact; reality; verity: as, a lover of ''truth'': often personified.
{{smaller block|<poem>“Sygrem,” he seid, “to yow I will not leyne,
I shall yow telle the ''trouth'' of this mater.”</poem>
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2284.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 332.}}}}

{{smaller block|You peradventure think aptness and ableness all one; whereas the ''truth'' is that, had we kept our first ableness, grace should not need. {{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v., App. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|For my mouth shall speak ''truth''. {{right|Prov. viii. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>For ''truth'' is ''truth''
To the end of reckoning.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., v. 1. 45.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Oh, ''Truth'', thou art a mighty conqueress!</poem>{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Queen of Corinth, iv. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Truth'' is the most unbending and uncompliable, the most necessary, firm, immutable, and adamantine thing in the world. {{right|''Cudworth'', Morality, IV. v. § 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Kant regards it as a duty owed to oneself to speak the ''truth'', because “a lie is an abandonment or, as it were, annihilation of the dignity of man.” {{right|''H. Sidgwick'', Methods of Ethics, p. 292.}}}}

(''b'') A verified fact; a true statement or proposition; an established principle, fixed law, or the like.

{{smaller block|Fundamental ''truths'', . . . like the lights of heaven, are not only beautiful and entertaining in themselves, but give light and evidence to other things that without them could not be seen. {{right|''Locke'', Conduct of the Understanding, § 43.}}}}

(''c'') That which is righteous or in accordance with the divine standard.

{{smaller block|He that doeth ''truth'' cometh to the light, that his deeds may be made manifest, that they are wrought in God. {{right|John iii. 21.}}}}

'''3†.''' Faith pledged; pledge; troth. See ''troth''.

{{smaller block|<poem>I’ll give thee the ''truth'' of my right hand;
The ''truth'' of it I’ll freely gie.</poem>{{right|''Young Beichan and Susie Pye'' (Child’s Ballads, IV. 4).}}}}

