mal, fundamental, gospel truth.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}—'''God’s truth.''' {{smaller|See ''God''<sup>1</sup>.}}—'''Immediate truths.''' {{smaller|See ''immediate'', 4.}}—'''In truth,''' {{smaller|truly; in fact; also, sincerely.}}—'''Logical, material, objective truth.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}}—'''Of a truth, of truth,''' {{smaller|in truth; in reality; certainly.}}
{{smaller block|Ffor ''of trewthe'' he ys not content with no man that ys famyliar with the company that ys at the Rodes, ffor that hell broude takys them as hys mortall enimies.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 22.}}
''Of a truth'' it is good to be with good people.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Virginians, xxiii.}}}}
'''Physical, pure, real, secondary, transcendental,''' {{smaller|etc.,}} '''truth.''' {{smaller|See the adjectives.}} ='''Syn.''' {{smaller|See ''reality''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''truth''' (tröth), ''v. t.'' [< ''truth'', ''n.''] To affirm or declare truthfully. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>The ancients
Who chatted of the golden age feigned trifles.
Had they dreamt this, they would have ''truth’d'' it heaven.</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Fancies, ii. 2.}}}}

'''truthful''' (tröth′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ''truth'' + ''-ful''.] '''1.''' Full of truth; habitually speaking the truth; veracious.
{{smaller block|The perfectly ''truthful'' man cannot entertain the proposal to say what is false.
{{right|''J. Sully'', Outlines of Psychol., p. 666.}}}}
'''2.''' Conformable to truth; correct; true: as, a ''truthful'' statement. ='''Syn.''' {{smaller|Sincere, honest, candid, frank, open, ingenuous, artless, guileless.}}

'''truthfully''' (tröth′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' In a truthful manner; with truth.

'''truthfulness''' (tröth′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' The character of being truthful: as, the ''truthfulness'' of a person or of a statement.

'''truthiness''' (trö′thi-nes), ''n.'' Truthfulness. [Rare.]

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>The kinges sone aswithe let sembul miche puple,
& ''triȝed'' him to a tidi ost of the tideȝist burnes.</poem>
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3556.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''b'') To ascertain by sifting or examination. ''Alisaunder of Macedoine'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 761.}}

{{smaller block|Master More was once sent in commission into Kent, to help to ''try'' out, if it might be, what was the cause of Goodwin Sands.
{{right|''Latimer'', Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1550.}}}}

'''2.''' To separate (metal) from the ore or dross by melting; refine; assay. [Not a technical use.]
{{smaller block|Silver ''tried'' in a furnace of earth, purified seven times.
{{right|Ps. xii. 6.}}
<poem>The fire seven times ''tried'' this;
Seven times ''tried'' that judgement is
That did never choose amiss.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., ii. 9. 63.}}}}

'''3.''' To separate or reduce by boiling or steaming; render: generally with ''out'': as, to ''try out'' lard or blubber.
{{smaller block|Aysell and wyne eke ''oute'' of hem men ''trie''.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 53.}}
''Palm.'' All my fat Oxen and Sheep are melted to this [money], Gentlemen.
''Whead.'' Their Grease is well ''try’d'', Sir.
{{right|''Etherege'', Love in a Tub, ii. 3.}}}}

'''4.''' To put to the test or proof; subject to experimental treatment, comparison with a standard, or the like, in order to determine the truth, accuracy, power, strength, speed, fitness, or other quality of; test; prove: as, to ''try'' weights and measures; to ''try'' a new invention; to ''try'' conclusions; to ''try'' one’s patience, or one’s luck.
{{smaller block|This word of God ''trieth'' all doctrine.
{{right|''J. Bradford'', Letters (Parker Soc., 1853), II. 9.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|It is a true Observation that, among other effects of Affliction, one is to ''try'' a Friend. {{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. vi. 55.}}

If God come to ''trie'' our constancy, we ought not to shrink, or stand the lesse firmly for that.
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, i. 7.}}

Had we no other way of ''trying'' the continuance of God’s goodness to us but by exercising his patience by our greater provocations? {{right|''Stillingfleet'', Sermons, I. i.}}

<poem>Your Goblin’s Skill shall now be ''try’d.''</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', An Impossible Thing.}}}}
'''5.''' To use, apply, or practise tentatively; experiment with: as, to ''try'' a new remedy; also, to experiment upon; treat tentatively.
{{smaller block|<poem>A bulbe of sqylle eke summen wol devyde,
And ther into this plannte of fig-tree ''trie'',
And bynde it so therto that it abyde.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 124.}}

He [a hare] was ill three days, during which time I nursed him, . . . and by . . . ''trying'' him with a variety of herbs restored him to perfect health.
{{right|''Cowper'', Treatment of Hares.}}

If that child were mine, Francis, I should ''try'' her with a little taraxacum.
{{right|''Mrs. Annie Edwardes'', Ought we to Visit her? xi.}}

The artist sometimes ''tried'' an attitude on a grouping, and then, dissatisfied with the effect, abandoned it.
{{right|''Harrison and Verrall'', Ancient Athens, p. cxi.}}}}
'''6.''' To endeavor experimentally to find out.
{{smaller block|<poem>We are four damsels sent abroad,
To the east, west, north, and south,
To ''try'' whose fortune is so good
To find these champions forth.</poem>
{{right|''Robin Hood and the Stranger'' (Child’s Ballads, V. 412).}}}}
